    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i'm just a bit paranoid about things going south under the surface. like if the bacteria/germs get deep into where i can't reach them, is there any way to know before she actually gets really sick? also just wondering if there's any reason why these kinds of infections seem to just 'lurk' without any warning.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "One telltale sign of a deep-seated infection is the presence of systemic signs such as fever, lethargy, or decreased milk production before you see any obvious changes at the wound site. This is often referred to as a \"pre-infectious\" presentation.\n\nAs for why infections can seem to \"lurk\" unnoticed, it's often due to the fact that early-stage infections may not produce visible signs, especially if they're not severely bacterial or the cow has some level of immune response. Additionally, bovine immunoglobulin (antibodies) and deep tissues can provide relative protection against surface-level bacterial invasion, allowing deeper infections to develop discreetly.",
      "source": "target",
